From 266f2f57c87b6bc35067c955f2eeddbb4efa7861 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 22:15:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Grade closeness instead of failing it, and build the
 sector fork

Daniil, on triangulated: closest available peers are fine, we do not need a
perfect match. He is right, and the flag was mislabelling its own data.
triangulated fired whenever no contributing row had one exact tag match, so
BrowserAct, whose comparables share ten words with it, was flagged identically to
Goldfish, whose share one generic one. Closeness is now graded SHARED_PRODUCT,
STRONG_OVERLAP, PARTIAL_OVERLAP, THIN_OVERLAP, and shared_words travels with it so
the reveal can print the overlap rather than assert it. Five of twenty sets are
thin, not seventeen.

selector/quiz_fork.py builds the fork Daniil signed off, cut to the metrics he
kept. One rule is enforced in code: unbacked() lists any question asking for a
metric we cannot show a peer figure for, and must stay empty. It caught one.

AI-native is a modifier rather than a fork. The first version sent 13 of 21
profiles down an AI branch that dropped the retention and margin questions;
OpenSEO and Context.dev are AI-native SaaS and being AI-native says nothing about
which yardstick applies. Nine to ten questions, six or seven required.

Untested: 19 of the 21 profiles take the software fork and 2 take e-commerce, so
marketplace, payments, lending, subscription and delivery have never run against a
real company. The extra test cases should be chosen to hit those five.
